course: dictionary
lesson: 4191
title: Old-fashioned words
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words sound old because people rarely use them today. «Goodly» is one of them. You will meet it in older books, old stories and the Bible, and a few set phrases still use it.
explain: Read the sentence first. «It is a goodly life that you lead, friend; no doubt the best in the world, if only you are strong enough to lead it!» The words «the best in the world» show the Water Rat is praising Mole's life, so goodly means fine or pleasant here. Check by swapping in your meaning: "a fine life, the best in the world" still makes sense.
explain: In everyday talk, people say «a goodly number» or «a goodly sum», and it means quite a large amount. Look at the word after goodly. A goodly number of people is a fair-sized crowd, not one or two. Old words can also make writing sound like it comes from long ago.
fact: Old-fashioned words are words people rarely use today, but they still appear in older books, stories and the Bible.
fact: «Goodly» is an adjective, so it describes a noun: a goodly number, a goodly sum, a goodly land.
fact: «Goodly» means quite a large amount, as in «a goodly number».
fact: In older writing, «goodly» can also mean good-looking, fine or pleasant, as in «a goodly land».
fact: Its comparison forms are «goodlier» and «goodliest», but they are rarely used today.
exercise: «A goodly number of children came to the party.» What does goodly mean here? | Late | Loud | Very small | *Quite a large amount
exercise: «It is a goodly life that you lead, friend; no doubt the best in the world.» What does goodly suggest? | Short and rude | Dull and boring | Very wet | *Fine and pleasant
exercise: «A goodly sum was raised for the school.» Which word tells us the sum is large? | school | sum | *goodly | raised
test: «The old book described a goodly land with green hills and clear rivers.» What does goodly mean here? | Very noisy | Dry and dusty | Tiny | *Fine and beautiful
test: Which phrase means quite a large number? | a tiny drop | *a goodly number | a wee bit | a few crumbs
exam: Which is the best way to work out what goodly means in an old story? | Guess the first word you see | *Read the words around it and swap in your meaning to check | Ignore the whole sentence | Skip it and never think about it again
